23:09:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: When we travel by plane or drive up a mountain, our ears often feel clogged because of rapid changes in air pressure. The ear has a small tube called the Eustachian tube that connects the middle ear to the back of the throat. Normally, it opens briefly to equalize the pressure inside your ear with the outside environment. If the pressure changes too quickly—like during takeoff, landing, or a fast mountain ascent—the tube may not open often enough, creating a pressure imbalance that makes the eardrum bulge. This gives the sensation of clogging or popping. In contrast, an elevator moves much more slowly, even in very tall buildings. The rate of pressure change is gradual, giving the Eustachian tube plenty of time to equalize. Also, the total altitude gain in a building (even a skyscraper) is far less than what you experience on a plane (thousands of feet) or a mountain (hundreds to thousands of meters), so the pressure difference is smaller and easier to manage. That’s why you rarely notice ear clogging in an elevator.
