ngs, no amount of forward thrust will keep you from falling. But your insight is correct: in a practical sense, for a typical jet airliner, if the engines fail, it will eventually glide down—it's not like it instantly falls out of the sky. That's because the plane still has forward speed (from momentum) and the wings still work. It just can't maintain altitude without power. **To summarize:** - **Thrust** keeps the plane moving forward (overcoming drag). - **Lift** keeps the plane from falling (overcoming weight). - The engine makes lift possible by providing forward speed, but it's the **wings** that actually do the lifting. And the fact that a glider flies without an engine proves that forward motion (from any source) + wings = flight. So the engine is not the ultimate reason—the wings are.
