23:59:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've raised a very sharp point. Sea turtles that eat jellyfish do face a significant salt load, because jellyfish are osmoconformers—their body fluids are roughly isotonic with seawater (about 1000 mOsm/L, similar salt concentration). So when a leatherback turtle eats a jellyfish, it's essentially swallowing seawater-packaged in a blob. ### How do they handle it without drinking freshwater? The answer lies in two specialized adaptations: 1. **Salt glands (lacrimal glands)**: Sea turtles have large salt-excreting glands located near their eyes. These glands actively pump out concentrated salt solution (even more concentrated than seawater) into tears, which then drip away. This is extremely efficient—they can excrete up to **90% of the ingested salt** this way, using very little water in the process. 2. **Highly efficient kidneys**: Their kidneys can also produce urine that is more concentrated than seawater, helping to remove any remaining salt. ### The net water balance When a leatherback turtle eats a jellyfish: - It ingests a large volume of water (jellyfish are ~95% water), but that water is nearly as salty as the ocean. - The salt glands remove the excess salt using **metabolic energy**, not by flushing with extra water. The tears are very salty (up to twice as salty as seawater), so the water lost in tears is far less than the water gained from the jellyfish. - Additionally, **metabolic water** from digesting the jellyfish's protein and fat adds more net water. The result: the turtle gets a **net gain of water** from eating jellyfish, even though the jellyfish itself is as salty as the sea. The salt glands and kidneys allow the turtle to "purify" the ingested water by removing the salt without losing a proportional amount of water. ### So, do they ever need freshwater? No—they are fully adapted to a life at sea without access to freshwater. All their water needs are met by their prey (even salty prey) and metabolic water. The salt