eding from them.** The only way that can be true is if there is no unique centre — the expansion is happening uniformly everywhere. ### The Big Bang "point" never existed in space The Big Bang wasn't an explosion that happened *somewhere in space*. It was the beginning of space and time themselves. All of space was infinitely dense and hot at that moment, and then it began to expand. That expansion continues today. So the "single point" you're thinking of isn't a location in the current universe — it's the entire universe itself, when it was incredibly tiny. And that tiny universe was everywhere, not just one spot. I hope that clears things up! The key is to stop thinking of galaxies as shrapnel from an explosion, and start thinking of them as raisins in a rising loaf of bread.
