00:34:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fair question, and it gets to the heart of how we know something is real even when it's too slow to see with a ruler. The answer comes from multiple lines of evidence, not just theory. ## 1. Direct measurement with a scale The simplest way to prove it is to use a kitchen scale. Pour a known amount of water into your dish, weigh it, leave it for a week, and weigh it again. The weight will decrease. A loss of even 0.1 mm depth in a 20 cm dish corresponds to about 3 grams of water—easily detectable on a scale with 1 g precision. If you do this, you'll see the drop. ## 2. Everyday examples you can verify - **Wet clothes on a drying rack.** If evaporation didn't happen at room temperature, clothes would stay wet forever indoors. But they dry completely, even in a cool room. That's evaporation. - **Sweat on your skin.** Your body uses evaporation of sweat to cool you. That works at 37°C, far below 100°C. - **A glass of water left for a month.** If you mark the level with a piece of tape, you'll see a noticeable drop after a month. It's just too slow to see in three days. ## 3. Thermodynamic necessity Water molecules at the surface are in constant motion. Even at 20°C, some have enough kinetic energy to overcome the attractive forces holding them in the liquid. That's not a theory—it's a statistical certainty. The rate is simply slow because the fraction of molecules with enough energy is small. ## 4. Why your ruler detected nothing The human eye and a standard ruler (1 mm markings) are not sensitive enough for three days at 20°C, 50% humidity, still air. In those conditions, the depth loss is maybe 0.1–0.2 mm *per week*, not per day. Over three days, that's less than 0.1 mm—far below what a ruler can measure. ## Your skepticism is healthy You're right to distrust claims that can't be verified. The solution is to use a more sensitive measurement. Try this: - Fill your shallow dish with water. - Weigh it on a kitchen scale to the nearest gram. - Leave it for one week in the same room. - Weigh it again. You will see a loss of several grams. That's your proof, not my words. If you still don't see a change after a week, then either your room is extremely humid (above 80%) or your scale is inaccurate. But under normal conditions, the weight loss is real and measurable. **In short:** The ruler test is not sensitive enough. The science is supported by countless experiments and everyday observations—but you can verify it yourself with a simple scale.
